    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **261 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that creating an owner in a city that already contains **50 or more** owners is rejected with **409 Conflict**, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `EnsureOwnerHouseholdUnique` pattern).\n\nNew/changed files:\n\n1. **`EnsureOwnerCityCapacity.java`** (function) \u2014 reads the validated request body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, counts existing owners whose city matches (case-insensitively) via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws a checked `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** \u2014 checked exception (extends `Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and routes through escalation.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `409 (Conflict)` with a `ProblemDetail`, matching the existing duplicate-household handler.\n\n4. **`officefloor/escalation/\u2026CityAtCapacityException.yml`** \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `ensureCityCapacity` step (under `[ transaction ]` governance) after the household-uniqueness check and before `build`, so a full city is a 409 rather than a persisted row.\n\n6. **Test** `createOwnerRejectsCityAtCapacity` in `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests` \u2014 seeds a fresh city with 50 owners (each with a distinct household and telephone so only the capacity rule can fire), then asserts the 51st create returns 409.\n\nThe check runs before `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner` on the read-of-request DTO, consistent with the other pre-persistence \"Ensure\" guards in the pipeline.",
